※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4203

※ A listable function stands where a plain one is wanted, dropping the walk: it
※ promises everything the plain one does and adds to it.  The other way round is
※ a promise nobody made -- the lambda was written without the word, so nothing
※ about it says an array handed to it means anything at all.
※
※ It is the rule `&mut T` follows where `&T` is wanted, and `&static T` where
※ `&T` is: a value that promises more may stand for one that promises less, one
※ way only.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let plain: fn(u8) → u8 = λ a: u8 → u8:
        a + a
    let walks: @[listable] fn(u8) → u8 = plain
    let v: u8⟦3⟧ = ⟦1u8, 2u8, 3u8⟧
    if walks(v)⟦0⟧ ≠ 2u8:
        1u6
    else:
        0u6
